index 48b478d..f8d18d3 100644
--- a/docs/source/console.rst
+++ b/docs/source/console.rst
@@ -69,6 +69,27 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a JSON string, with syntax highlighting. The output is valid JSON, indented with 4 spaces by default. You can change the indentation with the ``indent`` argument, and disable highlighting with ``highlight=False``. Here's an example::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Long lines are not wrapped. You can also log a JSON renderable with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`, or print it with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print`, by passing a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` instance::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.print(JSON('{"name": "rich"}'))
+
+If you don't have a console object, you can import the :func:`~rich.print_json` function from the ``rich`` package, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "rich"}', indent=2)
+
+Rich also has a command line entry point for pretty printing a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json PATH
+
